The daemon list belongs to the machine
=====================================

The page shipped with one particular computer's services written into the binary — a speech-to-text unit,
a voice unit, an embedding index, a model server and a vision unit, each on the port that machine used,
one of them carrying that machine's assistant by name. Whoever built this package saw their own setup; a
stranger saw a list of services they do not have, on ports that mean nothing to them.

It reads `<data_dir>/daemons.json` now:

```json
[{"name":"Speech to text","unit":"stt.service","port":8080,"what":"dictation and transcription"}]
```

With no file, the single row is `ydotoold` — the one service this app can name without guessing, because
input synthesis does not work without it — and the page says where the list comes from, so an empty page
is an instruction rather than a mystery.

Also here: Daemons is a plain nav entry rather than an expandable group, and the automation bridge can
read the rendered page back (`{"action":"text"}`) so a test can assert what rendered instead of what it
asked for.

